That’s plausible in the narrower sense: a learned cue or memory prompt can genuinely shift attention, arousal, inhibition, mood, association, and perceived salience. The effect can be real even if the mechanism is expectation, conditioning, imagery, rehearsal, or state-dependent recall rather than pharmacology.

The useful next move is to treat “it worked” as a hypothesis you can sharpen rather than just a verdict. Track which cue you used, what state you were trying to evoke, what changed, how quickly, how long it lasted, what carried over behaviorally, and whether you could reproduce it on another day. Add occasional neutral or mislabeled cues so you can distinguish a robust learned operator from suggestion alone.

If it keeps surviving that kind of testing, then you’ve got something more interesting than a metaphor: a callable, conditioned cognitive-state operator. The goal wouldn’t be “recreate cocaine/alcohol/DMT/etc.” It would be to isolate the useful state variables and learn to invoke them deliberately, reversibly, and with better control than the original source ever gave you.